Mesa 22.3.6: make the Gallium v3d driver work on V3D 3.3 hardware.

Mesa has carried the V3D 3.x code paths since the vc5 days, but they stopped
being exercised after Mesa 19: the Raspberry Pi 4's V3D is 4.2, and everything
older was only ever run against Broadcom's simulator.  Eleven defects, all
V3D-3.x-only, stand between Debian's Mesa and a working GPU on the BCM7268 -
the first five between it and a drawn triangle at all, the sixth between it and
a GPU that does not corrupt the kernel, the seventh a workaround rather than a
fix, the eighth between it and alpha blending, the ninth between it and
sampling linear buffers such as other clients' windows, the tenth between it
and texturing shaders that never finish, and the eleventh between it and
projective texture lookups, fixed-function texturing among them (checks/53):

1  nir_to_vir.c, vir_emit_last_thrsw(): a shader with no TMU access is dropped
   to a single thread, which emits no thread switch, and the function then
   dereferences the NULL c->last_thrsw.  The assert that guards it is compiled
   out in a release build, so every such shader crashed the process - which on
   V3D 3.x means every vertex shader.

2  qpu_schedule.c, pixel_scoreboard_too_soon(): a single-threaded shader never
   locks the scoreboard on a thread switch, so waiting for that lock never
   ends.  Restore the pre-lock-on-thrsw rule for it: no TLB access in the very
   first instruction.

3  vir.c, vir_uniform(): before V3D 4.x there is no ldunifrf, so a uniform can
   only be loaded into r5.  Load it there and copy it out at once, the way the
   SFU results in r4 are handled, so that several uniforms can be live at once.
   Upstream instead pins the ldunif temp to the r5 class and lets the register
   allocator spill when two interfere; that was tried on this hardware and every
   shader reading a uniform then rendered garbage, so this hunk is required.

4  nir_to_vir.c + v3d33_vpm_setup.c + v3d_compiler.h, vir_VPM_WRITE(): the V3D
   3.x branch carried a `/* XXX: v3d33_vir_vpm_write_setup(c); */` and wrote to
   the VPM with no write setup at all.  Mesa 19 emitted one setup per shader
   and wrote the outputs in slot order; 22.3 emits NIR store_output in whatever
   order it likes, and the 4.x path takes the slot per write (stvpmv), so give
   the 3.x setup an address parameter and emit one per write.

5  nir_to_vir.c, emit_vert_end()/emit_geom_end(): vpmwt was emitted only for
   V3D 4.0-4.2, as a workaround for GFXH-1684.  The instruction exists on V3D
   3.3 as well, and there it is not optional: without it the coordinate
   shader's VPM writes are not complete when the thread ends, so the PTB bins
   stale vertex data.  This is what made the hardware look dead - the binner
   allocated tile lists and the renderer executed them, but a job with a single
   triangle produced no pixels at all, while a job with dozens produced a
   window of about five primitives (one 16-vertex batch) at random.

6  v3d_job.c, v3d_job_submit(): tile_alloc and tile_state were added to the
   job's BO list only on the >= 4.1 path, so on V3D 3.3 nothing kept them
   alive while the GPU wrote its tile lists into them (see the hunk comment).

7  v3d_blit.c + v3d_screen.c: the TFU takes the whole SoC down on V3D 3.3.
   glGenerateMipmap on its own is enough - offscreen, no X, no compositor - and
   the console shows the texture L2 (client 0x0f by the 4.1 client table)
   reading a wild address, then the machine stops answering.  Mesa's TFU
   submission is unchanged from 19.0 apart from renames, so there is no older
   working version to restore and no upstream fix to copy; what this does
   instead is stop using the block on the generation where it does not work,
   the way this driver already handles its other pre-4.x gaps (PIPE_CAP_COMPUTE
   is gated on ver >= 41, v3d_tlb_blit returns early on ver < 40).  Both callers
   of v3d_tfu() already have a fallback: the state tracker draws the mipmap
   levels with util_gen_mipmap, and the blit path takes another route.  Mipmap
   generation costs ~4x more this way and the SoC stays up (checks/53 §9).

   This is a workaround, not a fix.  Why the TFU misbehaves here is not known.

8  v3d_packet_v33.xml, Blend Cfg: the packet is one opcode byte and a 32-bit
   payload on V3D 3.3, not 24 bits.  Broadcom's own driver for this chip
   (libv3ddriver.so on the stock rootfs, emitter at .text 0x9bb22) writes
   opcode 0x54 and four payload bytes, and its CL decoder carries a vg_mode
   field at bits 28-29.  Mesa's v3.3 definition ends at bit 23, so the packer
   emitted four bytes: the command-list parser took the next packet's opcode
   (Color Write Masks, 0x57) as the missing payload byte and then executed that
   packet's zero mask byte as HALT.  The binner stopped there (CT0CA was
   exactly one past that byte), the render job waited for tile lists that never
   came, and the kernel reset the GPU twice for every blended job - which is
   what glamor and glmark2 stormed on.  Declaring the vendor's field makes the
   packet the right length; Mesa leaves it at 0, which is also what the vendor
   writes on its normal path.

9  v3dx_emit.c, emit_one_texture(): the V3D 3.x texture state took its base
   address from psview->texture, the resource as the state tracker sees it.
   The TMU cannot sample raster (linear) memory, so v3d_create_sampler_view()
   gives a linear resource a tiled shadow copy in sview->texture, refreshes it
   before each draw, and ORs the shadow's layout bits into this very state - but
   the address still pointed at the linear original, so the TMU read raster
   memory as UIF.  Every imported linear buffer came out scrambled in 4x4
   utiles, which is how a GL client's window looked under glamor (DRI3 hands X
   the client's linear back buffer).  The V3D 4.x path,
   v3d_create_texture_shader_state_bo(), already uses so->texture; this does
   the same.  Measured with a linear GBM buffer sampled into an FBO: 89 992 of
   90 000 pixels wrong before, 0 after.

10 qpu_schedule.c, calculate_deps(): a TMU load (ldtmu) could be scheduled
   before the write that starts its lookup, and the shader then waits forever
   for a result that was never requested.  Until Mesa 21.0 every ldtmu had a
   write dependency on the last TMU write.  The 2021 scheduler work for V3D
   4.x (177dcd4b "be more flexible scheduling TMU writes", c3c251d9 "flag TMU
   reads with a read dependency on last TMU config", MRs 9555/9856) keeps
   ldtmu only after the last "sequence terminator", and
   tmu_write_is_sequence_terminator() lists only 4.x registers (TMUS, TMUSCM,
   TMUSF, TMUSLOD, TMUA, TMUAU).  On V3D 3.x the lookup starts with the TMUL
   write, so nothing orders ldtmu after it.  Whether the scheduler moves it up
   depends on register pressure.  glamor's composite shader, compiled for a
   BGRA render target (the R/B swap variant X uses for every depth-32
   pixmap), came out as `mov tmu ; ldtmu ; mov tmul`.  The fragment shader
   never ended, so the tile never finished: the render list ran to its end,
   and no frame was counted.  That was every one of the ~60 GPU resets a
   minute on the Xfce desktop.  This restores the pre-21.1 rule for V3D < 4.0
   only; 4.x keeps the new flexibility.

11 qpu_schedule.c, fixup_pipelined_ldvary() and qpu_merge_inst(): two ldvary
   signals could end up in one instruction, and one varying read vanished.
   Each ldvary reads the next varying component in order.  Both places refuse
   a second ldvary only through v3d_qpu_sig_writes_address(): on V3D 4.x
   ldvary writes an explicit register, so that catches it; on V3D 3.x ldvary
   writes r3/r5 implicitly, has no address, and the check passes.  The 2021
   ldvary pipelining (1784dd22 and follow-ups, tested on 4.x) then set ldvary
   on an instruction that already had one.  Mesa's fixed-function fragment
   shader reads TEX0 as s, t, r, q (r unused) for its projective lookup: 4
   ldvary in the VIR, 3 in the QPU code, q read as r = 0, 1/q infinite, and
   every texel taken from the texture's corner.  GLSL texture2DProj() failed
   the same way.  That was xfwm4's GLX compositor (vblank_mode auto) showing
   one solid colour instead of the desktop.  This makes both places refuse a
   second ldvary outright; on 4.x they already did.

Applied on top of Debian's mesa 22.3.6-1+deb12u2 source.

